      "raw_text": "Large-sample Properties of MLEs"
    }
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "MLEs have several important features when the sample size is large:",
      "source_page": 866,
      "printed_page": 846,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p048:b16",
        "Genetics_appendix1:p048:b17"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        145.0,
        1424.0,
        967.0,
        1458.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        866
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          146.0,
          1366.0,
          564.0,
          1402.0
        ],
        [
          145.0,
          1424.0,
          967.0,
          1458.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "c65976c264d6db9d28619eef79173d6b81e417d7f174750b48572c964d0baf44"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "1. Consistency: As the sample size increases, the MLE converges to the true parameter value, e.g., $ \\hat{\\Theta} \\to \\Theta $.",
      "source_page": 866,
      "printed_page": 846,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p048:b18"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        159.0,
        1468.0,
        1072.0,
        1539.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        866
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          159.0,
          1468.0,
          1072.0,
          1539.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "2520cf74de80392f171c3646b2952f1c6e64050bba2d6286731b9d0b09f018cb"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "2. Invariance: If $f(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})$ is a function of the unknown parameters of the distribution, then the MLE of $f(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})$ is $f(\\widehat{\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}})$, i.e., the MLE of a function of the parameters is simply that function evaluated at the MLE. For example, the MLE of $\\sqrt{\\theta} = (\\widehat{\\theta})^{1/2}$.",
      "source_page": 867,
      "printed_page": 847,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p049:b2"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        68.0,
        136.0,
        981.0,
        274.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        867
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          68.0,
          136.0,
          981.0,
          274.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "09e8610ebba6bf767a0cb979076ad9d35e7598530739eaaa2c9f62cc4473398d"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "3. Asymptotic normality and efficiency: As the sample size increases, the sampling distribution of the MLE converges to a normal and (generally) no other estimation procedure has a smaller variance. Hence, for sufficiently large sample sizes, estimates obtained via maximum likelihood typically have the smallest confidence intervals.",
      "source_page": 867,
      "printed_page": 847,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p049:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        69.0,
        285.0,
        982.0,
        448.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        867
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          69.0,
          285.0,
          982.0,
          448.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "0b8a75991aadaf45971efba96534bcc2ba9201f6de83d8302e52d98a8ae71a56"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "4. Variance: For large sample sizes, the variance of an MLE (assuming a single unknown parameter) is approximately the negative of the reciprocal of the second derivative of the log-likelihood function evaluated at the MLE $ \\widehat{\\theta} $,",
      "source_page": 867,
      "printed_page": 847,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p049:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        70.0,
        457.0,
        982.0,
        590.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        867
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          70.0,
          457.0,
          982.0,
          590.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "3aaf6317c2d86eb3e30cdc8aad9059242fab1718f8520dc89dd13419e01b0b3f"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula202]]",
      "source_page": 867,
      "printed_page": 847,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p049:b5",
        "Genetics_appendix1:p049:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        345.0,
        586.0,
